          "Q1. Which Rock Edict of Ashoka provides a direct, heartfelt account of the Kalinga War and his transformation from conquest by war to conquest by Dhamma?\n(a) Minor Rock Edict I\n(b) Major Rock Edict X\n(c) Major Rock Edict XII\n(d) Major Rock Edict XIII\nCorrect Answer: (d) Major Rock Edict XIII [Explanation: Major Rock Edict XIII describes the horrific casualties of the Kalinga War (100,000 slain, 150,000 captured) and declares Ashoka's remorse, announcing that true conquest is conquest by Dhamma.]",
          "Q2. The famous Junagadh Rock Inscription, composed in chaste, refined classical Sanskrit and recording the repair of the Sudarshana Lake dam, belongs to which Saka ruler?\n(a) Nahapana\n(b) Chashtana\n(c) Rudradaman I\n(d) Maues\nCorrect Answer: (c) Rudradaman I [Explanation: Dated to around 150 CE, the Junagadh inscription of Western Kshatrapa ruler Rudradaman I is the earliest major inscription in pure classical Sanskrit prose.]",
          "Q3. Who among the following ancient Indian mathematicians first calculated the value of Pi (\u03c0) to four decimal places (3.1416) and explained that solar and lunar eclipses are caused by planetary shadows?\n(a) Varahamihira\n(b) Brahmagupta\n(c) Aryabhata\n(d) Bhaskara I\nCorrect Answer: (c) Aryabhata [Explanation: In his astronomical treatise 'Aryabhatiya' (499 CE), Aryabhata calculated \u03c0 accurately and correctly theorized that eclipses are caused by the shadow of the earth falling on the moon or vice versa.]",
          "Q4. In the Sangam literature, which ecological land classification (Thinai) corresponds to coastal maritime lands inhabited by fishermen and salt-makers?\n(a) Kurinji\n(b) Mullai\n(c) Marudham\n(d) Neydhal\nCorrect Answer: (d) Neydhal [Explanation: In classical Tamil poetics, Neydhal denotes coastal sandy tracts associated with fishing and salt manufacturing; Kurinji is mountainous, Mullai is pastoral forest, and Marudham is fertile agricultural river plains.]",
