        "items": [
          "Q1. Which Article of the Indian Constitution provides constitutional protection against arbitrary dismissal or removal of members of civil services of the Union or a State?\n(a) Article 309\n(b) Article 310\n(c) Article 311\n(d) Article 312\nCorrect Answer: (c) Article 311 [Explanation: Article 311 provides that no civil servant shall be dismissed, removed, or reduced in rank without an inquiry where they are informed of charges and given a reasonable opportunity of being heard.]",
          "Q2. The National Green Tribunal (NGT) was established in 2010 under the provisions of which statute?\n(a) Environment (Protection) Act, 1986\n(b) National Green Tribunal Act, 2010\n(c) Wildlife (Protection) Act, 1972\n(d) Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980\nCorrect Answer: (b) National Green Tribunal Act, 2010 [Explanation: The NGT was established under the National Green Tribunal Act, 2010 for effective and expeditious disposal of cases relating to environmental protection.]",
          "Q3. The Competition Commission of India (CCI) was established under which Act of Parliament to prevent practices having adverse effects on market competition?\n(a) Monopolies and Restrictive Trade Practices (MRTP) Act, 1969\n(b) Competition Act, 2002\n(c) Consumer Protection Act, 2019\n(d) Companies Act, 2013\nCorrect Answer: (b) Competition Act, 2002 [Explanation: The Competition Act, 2002 established the Competition Commission of India (CCI) to eliminate anti-competitive practices, promote fair competition, and protect consumers.]",
          "Q4. 'Mission Karmayogi', launched by the Union Cabinet in 2020, is designed for:\n(a) Poverty alleviation in backward districts\n(b) Capacity building and bureaucratic reform of Indian Civil Servants\n(c) Providing collateral-free credit to artisans\n(d) Digitize urban land records\nCorrect Answer: (b) Capacity building and bureaucratic reform of Indian Civil Servants [Explanation: Mission Karmayogi (National Programme for Civil Services Capacity Building) focuses on behavioral and competency-based training of civil servants.]",
          "Q5. Under the Foreign Contribution (Regulation) Amendment Act, 2020, all NGOs receiving foreign contributions must maintain their designated FCRA bank account exclusively at:\n(a) Reserve Bank of India, Mumbai\n(b) State Bank of India, New Delhi Main Branch (Sansad Marg)\n(c) Any nationalized public sector bank\n(d) Punjab National Bank, Parliament Street\nCorrect Answer: (b) State Bank of India, New Delhi Main Branch (Sansad Marg) [Explanation: The 2020 FCRA amendment made it mandatory for all registered NGOs to receive foreign funds only through the designated SBI New Delhi Main Branch.]"
        ]
