        "style": "numbered",
        "items": [
          "Differentiate between Deontological (Duty-based) and Teleological (Consequentialist) ethical approaches. Illustrate how a civil servant can resolve conflicts between strict rule adherence and empathetic welfare delivery. (APPSC Group-1 Mains, 10 Marks)\n-> Model Approach: Define Deontology (Kant's duty for duty's sake regardless of outcomes) and Teleology/Utilitarianism (Bentham/Mill's greatest happiness of the greatest number); discuss typical administrative dilemmas: e.g., an impoverished elderly widow lacking an Aadhaar card seeking subsidized food rations under the PDS; analyze the limits of rigid rule adherence (leading to starvation) versus unguided utilitarian discretion (leading to audit objections); advocate for administrative discretion guided by Constitutional Morality and compassionate equity within statutory limits (e.g., using emergency exemption clauses under PDS control orders).",
          "Explain the Seven Nolan Principles of Public Life. Why is 'Selflessness' considered the foundational virtue of public administration? (APPSC Group-1 Mains, 10 Marks)\n-> Model Approach: Detail Lord Nolan's 1995 Committee report; enumerate and define all 7 principles: Selflessness, Integrity, Objectivity, Accountability, Openness, Honesty, Leadership; elaborate on Selflessness as the bedrock antidote against conflict of interest, rent-seeking, and administrative greed; explain how selflessness aligns with Kautilya's Yogakshema and Mahatma Gandhi's concept of Trusteeship in public governance.",
          "Examine the conflict between ethics in private relationships and ethics in public relationships with suitable administrative examples. (APPSC Group-1 Mains, 10 Marks)\n-> Model Approach: Contrast the particularistic loyalty and emotional bias legitimate in family life with the universalistic neutrality and impartiality required in public service; analyze real-world ethical dilemmas: a public procurement committee where a family member submits the lowest commercial bid; recusal mechanisms to uphold the principle of 'justice must not only be done, but seen to be done'; emphasize codes of conduct requiring public declaration of private assets and relationships."
        ]
