          "Examine the legal challenges encountered in determining the territorial jurisdiction of courts in cyber-crime cases. Discuss the relevant extra-territorial provisions of the Information Technology Act, 2000. (APPSC Group-1 Mains, 10 Marks)\n-> Model Approach: Define the borderless, transnational, and anonymous nature of cyberspace conflicting with traditional physical territoriality (Section 177 CrPC); analyze Section 75 of the IT Act (applying to offenses committed outside India by any person if target involves a computer system located in India); detail operational roadblocks: use of darknet, multi-hop proxy VPNs, servers located in hostile foreign jurisdictions, procedural delays in Mutual Legal Assistance Treaties (MLAT), and lack of standardized global cloud data extradition frameworks (e.g., Budapest Convention debate); conclude with reforms: specialized cyber-crime courts, international treaties, and cloud data preservation protocols.",
          "Discuss the significance of the landmark Supreme Court ruling in Shreya Singhal v. Union of India (2015). Why was Section 66A of the IT Act struck down? (APPSC Group-1 Mains, 10 Marks)\n-> Model Approach: Outline the factual context of Section 66A penalizing 'offensive' electronic messages; analyze grounds for invalidation by a 2-judge bench: (1) Overbreadth and vagueness violating Article 19(1)(a), failing the clear and present danger test, (2) Not protected under reasonable restrictions of Article 19(2), (3) Creating a 'chilling effect' on free speech; highlight how Section 69A (website blocking) was upheld with procedural safeguards; discuss contemporary enforcement issues where police continued invoking the dead section until the SC's 2022 directives.",
          "Analyze the structural advantages and federal friction points of the Goods and Services Tax (GST) regime in India. (APPSC Group-1 Mains, 10 Marks)\n-> Model Approach: Detail economic advantages: elimination of tax cascading via Input Tax Credit, creation of a unified common national market, digitization through GSTN reducing evasion, growth of monthly revenues (>\u20b91.7 lakh crore); analyze federal friction points: erosion of state fiscal sovereignty (loss of independent sales tax autonomy), dispute over the expiry of the 5-year GST compensation cess window in June 2022, Union veto power in GST Council (holding 33.3% vote where 75% is required); conclude on strengthening dispute resolution and streamlining inverted duty structures."
