          "Explain the mechanism of CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing technology. How does the 2022 deregulation of SDN-1 and SDN-2 genome-edited plants impact agricultural biotechnology in India? (APPSC Group-1 Mains, 10 Marks)\n-> Model Approach: Define CRISPR-Cas9 as an adaptive bacterial immune defense adapted for precision genome engineering; explain components: guide RNA (gRNA) matching the target sequence and Cas9 endonuclease introducing targeted double-stranded breaks; distinguish between Site-Directed Nuclease types: SDN-1 (small insertions/deletions via non-homologous end joining without foreign DNA), SDN-2 (few base changes using donor template without foreign DNA), and SDN-3 (insertion of large foreign DNA sequences/transgenes); explain the significance of the 2022 MoEFCC notification exempting SDN-1 and SDN-2 from stringent GM regulations under Rules 1989; evaluate outcomes: accelerated development of climate-resilient, pest-resistant crops without prolonged transgenic controversy.",
          "Critically examine the balance struck by the Indian Patents Act, 1970 between rewarding pharmaceutical innovation and ensuring affordable public healthcare access. Discuss with reference to Section 3(d) and the landmark Novartis ruling. (APPSC Group-1 Mains, 10 Marks)\n-> Model Approach: Trace the historical backdrop: transition from product patents to process patents under the 1970 Act, and return to product patents post-TRIPS (2005 amendment); analyze Section 3(d) designed to curb 'evergreening'\u2014preventing multinational pharmaceutical companies from extending 20-year monopolies by patenting minor crystal, salt, or ester variations of known compounds; detail the Supreme Court's ruling in Novartis AG v. Union of India (2013) which rejected patent protection for the beta-crystalline form of Imatinib Mesylate (Glivec) due to failure to prove enhanced therapeutic efficacy; discuss Compulsory Licensing (Section 84, Natco-Bayer Nexavar case) and its role in protecting India's title as the 'Pharmacy of the Developing World'.",
