          "Q1. Section 3(d) of the Indian Patents Act, 1970, which was at the centre of the landmark Novartis Glivec judgment, strictly prohibits the patenting of:\n(a) Biological inventions using plant seeds\n(b) Mere discovery of a new form of a known substance that does not enhance therapeutic efficacy (preventing evergreening)\n(c) Inventions related to atomic energy\n(d) Mathematical methods and business software algorithms\nCorrect Answer: (b) Mere discovery of a new form of a known substance that does not enhance therapeutic efficacy (preventing evergreening) [Explanation: Section 3(d) was introduced to prevent evergreening of pharmaceutical patents by requiring proof of significantly improved therapeutic efficacy.]",
          "Q2. The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2020 was awarded to Emmanuelle Charpentier and Jennifer A. Doudna for the development of which revolutionary biotechnology method?\n(a) Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)\n(b) CRISPR-Cas9 method for genome editing\n(c) Direct DNA sequencing\n(d) Monoclonal antibody production\nCorrect Answer: (b) CRISPR-Cas9 method for genome editing [Explanation: Charpentier and Doudna discovered one of gene technology\u2019s sharpest tools: the CRISPR/Cas9 genetic scissors.]",
