          "Q1. The difference between Compound Interest and Simple Interest on a certain sum of money for 2 years at 10% per annum is \u20b965. What is the principal sum?\n(a) \u20b96,000\n(b) \u20b96,500\n(c) \u20b97,000\n(d) \u20b97,500\nCorrect Answer: (b) \u20b96,500 [Explanation: Using Diff_2 = P * (R/100)^2: 65 = P * (10/100)^2 = P * (1/10)^2 = P / 100 => P = 65 * 100 = \u20b96,500.]",
          "Q2. A dishonest shopkeeper professes to sell his goods at cost price, but uses a false weight of 950 grams for a 1 kg weight. What is his actual gain percentage?\n(a) 5%\n(b) 5.26%\n(c) 5.55%\n(d) 6.25%\nCorrect Answer: (b) 5.26% [Explanation: Gain % = [Error / (True Value - Error)] * 100 = [50 / (1000 - 50)] * 100 = (50 / 950) * 100 = 100 / 19 \u2248 5.26%.]",
          "Q3. Two successive discounts of 20% and 15% are equivalent to a single net discount of:\n(a) 32%\n(b) 35%\n(c) 30%\n(d) 38%\nCorrect Answer: (a) 32% [Explanation: Net discount = a + b - (ab/100) = 20 + 15 - (20 * 15 / 100) = 35 - 3 = 32%.]",
          "Q4. A person sells two articles for \u20b99,900 each. On one he gains 10% and on the other he loses 10%. What is his overall gain or loss percentage on the entire transaction?\n(a) No profit no loss\n(b) 1% Gain\n(c) 1% Loss\n(d) 2% Loss\nCorrect Answer: (c) 1% Loss [Explanation: When two items are sold at the same Selling Price with equal percentage gain and loss x%, there is always an overall loss given by Loss % = (x / 10)^2 % = (10 / 10)^2 = 1% loss.]",
